The other languages' programs that `zap test --only bench` (../bench.zap) races against the Zap
programs in tests/benchmarks/, one file per problem and language: <problem>.c, <problem>.cpp,
<problem>.rs (and <problem>.rs.Cargo.toml when the Rust program uses crates).

Programs for nbody, spectralNorm, fannkuchRedux, binaryTrees, fasta, mandelbrot, kNucleotide and
revComp come from The Computer Language Benchmarks Game
(https://benchmarksgame-team.pages.debian.net/benchmarksgame/), each the fastest of its language
that builds here, with its header naming its authors kept. The only changes are for Windows:
stdin and stdout in binary mode (_setmode), and the processor count read from
NUMBER_OF_PROCESSORS where the program called sysconf, and `unsigned long long` where
mandelbrot.c meant a 64-bit `unsigned long` (32 bits on Windows); revComp.rs takes the raw
stdin and stdout handles on Windows as it does on Unix (io::stdout() writes line by line).
For Linux, x86-64 and AArch64: the Windows-only code (io.h, _setmode, windows.h, Winsock) is under
#ifdef _WIN32 with the POSIX code beside it, and the processor count is sysconf's / omp_get_num_procs()
there. Where a program uses x86 SIMD, the x86 code is as published and AArch64 runs the same algorithm
with NEON: x86_on_neon.h (the SSE2, SSE4.1 and AVX operations these programs use, written over NEON,
each doing what its namesake does) for nbody, spectralNorm and fannkuchRedux in C and C++, a copy of
the few operations in the Rust files (nbody.rs, spectralNorm.rs, fannkuchRedux.rs), NEON table lookups
beside the SSE ones in revComp.c and revComp.cpp, and a vector typedef for mandelbrot.c; fasta.rs,
revComp.rs and mandelbrot.cpp already had portable paths for it. Also: fannkuchRedux.cpp's
small_int_t is `signed char` (char is unsigned on AArch64), fasta.cpp takes the array size from its
type (g++ 13 does not accept p.size() of a parameter as a constant), nbody.cpp includes <cstdlib>, and
regexRedux.c and regexRedux.cpp use libpcre2-8 through pcre2.h (-lpcre2-8). The flags of the table
that only one host has are chosen by the host (-lws2_32 on Windows, -lpcre2-8 elsewhere), and
mandelbrot's -mno-fma, which only x86 has, is -ffp-contract=off (the same: no fused multiply-add).
khash.h is klib's hash table
(https://github.com/attractivechaos/klib, MIT license, its header kept), which kNucleotide.c
includes. Those programs are under this license:

