The programs of the other problems were written for this repository and are under its MIT license.

C++ sources (the .cpp files):
- nbody.cpp: Benchmarks Game "n-body C++ g++ #0"; spectralNorm.cpp: "spectral-norm C++ g++ #6" (the processor count from
  NUMBER_OF_PROCESSORS); fannkuchRedux.cpp: "fannkuch-redux C++ g++ #6" (OpenMP: it runs parallel only with -fopenmp);
  fasta.cpp: "fasta C++ g++ #7" (#9, the fastest, needs Boost); revComp.cpp: "reverse-complement C++ g++ #2" (SIMD defined).
  Besides the Windows changes, fasta.cpp and fannkuchRedux.cpp add a standard header newer compilers need.
- nsieve.cpp, matMul.cpp, base64.cpp, wordFreq.cpp, jsonRecords.cpp: written for this repository.
- regexRedux.c and regexRedux.cpp: written for this repository over PCRE2 with its JIT (the published C
  program's library). WinLibs ships libpcre2-8-0.dll but neither pcre2.h nor an import library, so each
  declares the few functions it calls and loads them from the DLL at start-up; std::regex is orders
  of magnitude too slow here and boost is not in the toolchain.

Changed for the Zap benchmarks game's rules (problems/problems.zap):
- nsieve.py: the sieve takes one bit per number (it took a byte per number).

Python programs (CPython 3, standard library only; run as `python -I`): nbody.py (game Python 3
#1), spectralNorm.py (#4), fannkuchRedux.py (#4), binaryTrees.py (#4), fasta.py (#5),
mandelbrot.py (#7), kNucleotide.py (#3) and revComp.py (#5) are the game's fastest entries, their
headers kept. Changed for Windows and the rules: LF line endings on stdout; kNucleotide shares the
sequence with its worker processes through a Pool initializer (no fork); mandelbrot computes each
point's coordinates as the C program does, escapes on re*re+im*im > 4.0 and masks each row's last
byte. base64.py, wordFreq.py, jsonRecords.py, dijkstra.py, raytrace.py, exprTree.py and regexRedux.py
(re) were written for this repository, as was regexRedux.rs (the regex crate).
